        `NullSafeMutableLiveData` is disabled because the detector crashes.

        What happens: `androidx.lifecycle` ships a lint jar containing
        `NonNullableMutableLiveDataDetector`, compiled against a Kotlin Analysis
        API in which `KaCallableMemberCall` is an interface. AGP 8.7.3 bundles an
        older Analysis API in which it is a class. When the detector runs it dies
        with `IncompatibleClassChangeError`, and lint reports that as
        "Unexpected failure during lint analysis (this is a bug in lint or one of
        the libraries it depends on)".

        This is a known AGP 8.7.x incompatibility, not a nova-tv defect. The
        release build fails because `assembleRelease` runs `lintVitalRelease`,
        which hits the same crash -- so a tooling bug in an unrelated detector
        was blocking the entire release artefact.

        Why disabling it costs nothing here: the detector looks for nullable
        `MutableLiveData` being observed unsafely. This project contains no
        LiveData of any kind -- it uses `StateFlow` and `collectAsStateWithLifecycle`
        throughout. A grep for `LiveData` across src/ returns nothing. The
        detector has no input, so no coverage is lost.

        The real fix is aligning AGP with the lifecycle lint jar (AGP 8.10.1+
        bundles a matching Analysis API). That is a toolchain-wide change, and
        this app's only untested component should not be the build stack. When
        AGP is upgraded, delete this entry and let the detector run.
    -->
    <issue id="NullSafeMutableLiveData" severity="ignore" />

